WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:17.130
In 1874, a naturalist by the name of Joseph
Leidy took out his microscope and examined

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.770
some water from a local Pennsylvania spring.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:27.640
On the other side of his lenses, he found
a creature that looked a bit like this one.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:33.660
Leidy documented the creature in his microscopy
magnum opus “Fresh-water Rhizopods of North

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:40.320
America,” which includes beautiful images
produced through chromolithography, an early

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.359
method of color printing, based on what he’d
seen.

00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:50.890
And just like you might, he had some assumptions
about what he thought this thing was.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:57.980
To Leidy, the first glimpses seemed to suggest
a giant amoeba dragging around mycelia, those

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.550
threads that fungi use to spread and gather
nutrients.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:07.390
But the more he found other creatures like
this, the more he considered that those threads

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:13.600
were a part of the amoeba itself—that perhaps
they were part of a strange new structure,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.890
and this was a strange new amoeba.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:24.000
While observations were rare, Leidy was able
to find enough of his own samples, as well

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.640
as descriptions from another scientist who
had found a similar species.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.110
And so Leidy named a new species Ouramoeba
vorax.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:40.490
But he still couldn’t quite let go of how
much those threads looked like mycelium.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:46.510
In Fresh-water Rhizopods, he wrote
I have repeatedly recognized, among the food

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:54.640
of various Amoeba, different kinds of fungus-spores,
and it is not unlikely that these lowly creatures

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:01.520
may be infested with fungus-parasites, just
as we frequently find to be the case with

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.340
insects.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:10.560
But he was not quite convinced of his fungal
hypothesis either, favoring the idea that

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.730
this threaded amoeba was its own species.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:19.360
And so the Ouramoeba vorax was documented,
illustrated, and examined.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:26.230
We’re narrowing in, right now, on one very
small part of Joseph Leidy’s life, and we’re

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:31.580
about to talk about why he was wrong about
Ouramoeba vorax and should have followed this

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.650
mycelial thread a bit further.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:42.380
But first, a brief moment to note that Joseph
Leidy did incredible work in both paleontology

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.240
and microscopy.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.950
There are even reports that he was the first
person to use a microscope for forensic purposes.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:56.000
But intelligence is not the absence of mistakes,
and as Leidy wrote in the introduction to

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.870
Fresh-water Rhizopods of North America:
While I have endeavored to describe things

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:07.420
as they appear to be, I am conscious of having
been unable to avoid the usual proportion

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:13.980
of errors, for which I beg indulgence, and
which I leave for others who shall pursue

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.930
the same path of investigation to correct.”

00:03:18.105 --> 00:03:20.105
Good call man!

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:25.300
In the case of the Ouramoeba vorax, it would
take several decades for someone to correct

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.830
Leidy’s mistake.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:33.360
In 1902, the Swiss microbiologist Eugène
Penard published his own extensive work on

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.270
protozoans: “The Rhizopods of the Léman
Basin”.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:41.980
And when it came to this particular creature,
he quibbled with Leidy’s quibbles with a

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.310
fungal explanation.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:51.140
As far as Penard could see, those filaments
had to be the result of some kind of parasite.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:56.860
And the observations he and later scientists
would make have continued to support that

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.090
what we are watching is a fungus in action.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:06.760
More specifically, we are watching a fungus
called Amoebophilus simplex infect an amoeba.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:12.390
The infection might have started with a fungal
spore getting attached to an amoeba, or perhaps

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.540
the spore was part of a meal.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:20.780
Well, once inside the amoeba, the spores migrate
to one end and begin to form those chains

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.199
that Leidy observed.

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:28.639
They’re called haustoria, and they flower
through the amoeba’s membrane to create

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:33.919
a thread of new spores that will eventually
be released into the environment to find a

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:35.460
new host.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:42.930
We here are really lucky to be able to see
Amoebophilus simplex in action, even if it’s

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.090
at the amoeba’s expense.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.270
This fungus is quite rare.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:53.320
And it seems to infect only a few amoeba species,
which made it difficult for researchers to

00:04:53.320 --> 00:05:00.960
study how the fungus works and how it compares
to other amoeba-infecting fungi.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.930
And those other fungi are also mysterious
and weird in their own ways!

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:12.099
James, our master of microscopes, found this
giant amoeba on the same slide where the Amoebophilus

00:05:12.099 --> 00:05:15.949
simplex fungus was infecting other smaller
amoeba.

00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:18.570
So James set the slide in a humidity chamber.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.409
And if you’re like the rest of us on the
Microcosmos team, maybe you’ve wondered

00:05:22.409 --> 00:05:26.379
what a humidity chamber even is because it
sounds kind of fancy.

00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:31.000
Well, it turns out it’s a Tupperware container
with wet cotton balls lining the bottom.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.689
Because sometimes the simplest tools are the
most effective.

00:05:35.689 --> 00:05:40.440
And when James put his slide in the humidity
chamber, he didn’t expect anything very

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.699
dramatic to happen.

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:44.539
And for a week, nothing did.

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:50.249
The Amoebophilus simplex continued to infect
the smaller amoebas crawling on the slide,

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:53.289
but the giant amoebas were still healthy.

00:05:53.289 --> 00:06:00.669
Until a week later, when he was casually checking
the slide to see the infected ones and found

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:06.879
that the giant amoebas were stuffed with a
bunch of tiny spores that looked similar to

00:06:06.879 --> 00:06:09.569
the ooomycetes we’ve seen before.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:16.220
Now, we don’t know what this fungus is,
just that after James found one giant amoeba

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:21.539
filled up with spores cases, he found another
one, and then another one!

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:28.879
And even more oddly, the amoeba began to melt
away and lay the spherical spore cases like

00:06:28.879 --> 00:06:30.680
they were eggs.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:37.169
And after several minutes, those spore cases
melted away to release more spores.

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:40.680
Now, the spores didn’t seem to go anywhere.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.870
After several days, they were still just waiting
on the slide.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.979
Maybe they’re like Amoebophilus simplex,
just waiting for the right host to come along

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:49.979
and eat them.

00:06:49.979 --> 00:06:55.439
Or maybe there’s something else going on
that we don’t know about and haven’t accounted

00:06:55.439 --> 00:06:56.439
for.

00:06:56.439 --> 00:07:02.289
After all, to steal a wise microscopist’s
words: “While [we] have endeavored to describe

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:09.669
things as they appear to be, [we are] conscious
of having been unable to avoid the usual proportion

00:07:09.669 --> 00:07:15.949
of errors, for which [we] beg indulgence,
and [we] leave to others who shall pursue

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:19.250
the same path of investigation to correct”

00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:31.080
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.169
And thank you, especially, to all of the people
